    }
  ]
}

{
  "subevents": [
    {
      "id": "0",
      "subevent": "President Trump pointed to a bar chart showing one country's coronavirus death toll per capita stretched out beyond all others, which was Belgium."
    },
    {
      "id": "1",
      "subevent": "The graph placed the U.S. rate below six European nations."
    },
    {
      "id": "2",
      "subevent": "Belgian minister Denis Ducarme called Trump's display 'macabre' and 'level zero' politics."
    },
    {
      "id": "3",
      "subevent": "Ducarme tweeted that Belgium's method of counting COVID-19 deaths is the most exhaustive possible."
    },
    {
      "id": "4",
      "subevent": "Belgium includes deaths suspected of being virus-related, whether the victim was tested or not."
    },
    {
      "id": "5",
      "subevent": "As of Wednesday, Belgium counted 6,262 deaths from COVID-19, roughly 540 per million citizens."
    },
    {
      "id": "6",
      "subevent": "More than half of those deaths were in nursing homes; of those, only 4.5% were confirmed infected, yet all are counted in the national tally."
    },
    {
      "id": "7",
      "subevent": "Belgian authorities faced pressure to change their counting method due to negative perception."
    },
    {
      "id": "8",
      "subevent": "Prime Minister Sophie Wilmès suggested the count has been exaggerated."
    },
    {
      "id": "9",
      "subevent": "Emmanuel André, spokesman for the government's coronavirus task force, defended the method and said other countries are copying the Belgian surveillance model."
    },
    {
      "id": "10",
      "subevent": "André said, 'When you don't know, it's more difficult to act.'"
    },
    {
      "id": "11",
      "subevent": "Experts at Wednesday's briefing said the country likely hit the coronavirus' peak on April 12."
    },
    {
      "id": "12",
      "subevent": "On Friday, a special task force is due to unveil a gradual exit strategy from lockdown measures."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "subevents": [
    {
      "id": "0",
      "subevent": "The Technical University of Denmark shut down, but Professor Anders Wolff continues working on the CoronaDX project to develop three new measuring instruments for fast on-site coronavirus diagnosis."
    },
    {
      "id": "1",
      "subevent": "Project partner Anders Fomsgaard from Statens Seruminstitut also continues working because two instruments are scheduled for emergency use in October and the third in March 2021."
    },
    {
      "id": "2",
      "subevent": "Several research teams are working on a vaccine, but approval is 1-2 years away, so it is essential to develop technology to quickly separate healthy from infected people."
    },
    {
      "id": "3",
      "subevent": "The fastest instrument will give diagnosis in less than one minute, the slowest in less than an hour, and all three are designed to be very easy to use, like a pregnancy test, for use in clinics or at home."
    },
    {
      "id": "4",
      "subevent": "On-site testing saves time and resources, decreases pressure on central laboratories, and reduces risk of hospital staff infection by enabling immediate quarantine."
    },
    {
      "id": "5",
      "subevent": "Quarantine-affected persons can get immediate diagnosis and resume normal lives if not infected, keeping more people working during an epidemic."
    },
    {
      "id": "6",
      "subevent": "Professor Fomsgaard, responsible for two instruments including the fastest, says the speed and ease could increase healthcare system effectiveness and become a new tool for diagnosing viral diseases."
    },
    {
      "id": "7",
      "subevent": "Professors Wolff and Fomsgaard expect to have emergency use permit in Denmark by October for the possible second wave of coronavirus."
    },
    {
      "id": "8",
      "subevent": "Under normal conditions development would take years, but Wolff says the research community is collaborating across countries rather than competing, enabling fast progress."
    },
    {
      "id": "9",
      "subevent": "The three instruments use three different measurement methods and the technology is the newest in the field, recently developed in an EU project for poultry diseases."
    },
    {
      "id": "10",
      "subevent": "Wolff says the measuring instrument is ready but new reagents are needed to react with the virus; emergency regulations allow field testing earlier than usual."
    },
    {
      "id": "11",
      "subevent": "As early as June, two Chinese partners will test two of the instruments in the field in China."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "subevents": [
    {
      "id": "0",
      "subevent": "France seizes British scallop dredger Cornelis Gert Jan and escorts it to Le Havre."
